R"VG(
# medieval11.vg (H-04; the second pass 2026-10-10): the ballad (an event sung, the song register: song*.vg; here its opening, a call
# to the instruments and the cups, and what is left after) and the sermon's teaching: its faith's own words, a story told for a lesson.

# ---- the ballad: an event, sung ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.ballad (words=31..120)
  (2) {ballad.open}\n{@event battle,sack,siege,war,sickness,flood,famine,quake,eruption,rulerdeath,death,founding,built,omen,impact,fire,storm,wonder,games,accident,fair,trial,contact song 40}\n{ballad.close}
  {ballad.open}\n{med.bal.strike}\n{@event battle,sack,siege,war,sickness,flood,famine,quake,eruption,rulerdeath,founding,built,omen,impact,fire,storm,wonder,games,accident,fair,trial,contact song 40}\n{ballad.close}
  {ballad.open}\n{@event battle,sack,siege,war,sickness,flood,famine,quake,eruption,rulerdeath,death,founding,built,omen,impact,fire,storm,wonder,games,accident,fair,trial,contact song 40}\n{med.bal.after}\n{ballad.close}

ballad.open (formula)
  Listen, and I will sing it as it was.
  This is the song they sing at {city} when the cups are full.
  Sit, all of you, and hear what happened.
  Sing it slowly; it is long, and it is true.
  Here is a song of {$home}, as my mother sang it.
  Put down the {drink}, and listen.
  (age>=industrial) This is the old song, as it is sung at {$home}; the singer is {med.bal.singer}.

med.bal.singer
  blind, and better for it
  the miller's daughter
  a carter who knows every verse
  very old, and you can hear it

ballad.close (formula)
  So it was, and so we sing it.
  That is the song. Fill the cups.
  And that is why we sing it still, in {season.now}, at {festival}.
  That is all of it. The rest the old ones took with them.
  So ends the song. Now someone else sing.

# the instruments and the hall, before the song (a line or two)
med.bal.strike (share=0.4)
  Strike {instrument}, and let the {drink} go round,\nfor this is the song of {$home}.
  Bring the lamp nearer, and {instrument} with it;\nthe song is old and needs the light.
  Let the young ones sit at the front,\nthey have not heard it, and they should.
  {instrument}, slowly; slowly, the voice;\nthis is a song for {season.now}.
  Clear the {food} away and the {vessel} with it;\nthere is no eating in this song.
  Somebody keep the fire, somebody keep the door,\nand everybody keep quiet.
  Under the {roofs} of {$home} we sing it,\nas they sang it under theirs.
  (coast=1) Sing it the way the boatmen sing it,\nwith the oars in it.
  Put the {herd} in for the night and come in yourselves;\nthe song will not wait for stragglers.
  Pour the {drink} for the singer first,\nand for whoever plays {instrument} after.
  The {craft} can wait till morning,\nand so can the quarrel about the {med.chr.quarrel.s}.
  Hang up the {colour.holy} cloth behind the singer,\nthe way it was hung the first time.
  Bank the {fuel} and close the shutters,\nand let {weather} listen from outside.
  Let the old woman who knows it start it,\nand the rest of you come in on the turn.

# what is left after the song (a line or two; `$e` the event sung, where there was one)
med.bal.after (share=0.5)
  (has:$e.place) And still at {$e.place} the {bird} cry over the stones,\nand nobody knows what they are saying.
  (has:$e.place) Go to {$e.place} now and you will find {med.bal.left},\nand nothing to tell you what happened there.
  And the ones who sang it first are {dead.are},\nand the song is here.
  And {time.pass},\nand we sing it in {season.now} when the work is done.
  And the {crop} grew again over all of it,\nas the {crop} does.
  And the children ask if it is true,\nand we say it is truer than the truth.
  (has:$e.place) And the {herd} graze at {$e.place} where it happened,\nand do not know, and do not need to.
  And the {craft}-women of {$home} wove it into the {colour.holy} cloth,\nwhich is how we still know the order of it.
  And every {festival} somebody weeps at this verse,\nand somebody else fills the cup.

med.bal.left
  {crop} growing
  {herd} grazing
  a {tree.n} and a stone
  {med.houses}
  nothing but the wind

med.houses
  (plan=round age<=medieval) round houses
  (plan=square age<=medieval) long houses
  (plan=court) houses round their courts
  (land=stilts) houses on stilts
  (age>=industrial) new houses
  houses of {walls.of}

# the faith's own teaching (by the people's faith; its rite's), each people its own half
med.ser.faith (share=0.5)
  (faith=ancestors) The old ones did not leave us. They went ahead, the way a parent goes ahead on the road to {city2}, to see.
  (faith=ancestors) Every name we say at {festival} is a lamp in a dark house. Say them, all of them, even the ones you did not like.
  (faith=ancestors) Your mother's mother sat where you sit. She was tired too, and her {herd} were thinner. She came anyway.
  (faith=ancestors) The old ones planted the {tree} on the hill so that we would have shade they never sat in. Plant something.
  (faith=ancestors) When you eat {food} at {festival}, set a bowl for the old ones. They do not eat it; that is not the point.
  (faith=ancestors) The old ones are {dead.are}, and they know the price of {crop} better than the {med.chr.trades} do.
  (faith=ancestors) We are the old ones of somebody not born yet. Behave like it.
  (faith=land) {god} is in {land} and in the {crop} and in the water of {river}. Walk softly; you are walking on {god}.
  (faith=land) The land does not belong to us. We belong to it, and it has been patient with us.
  (faith=land) Every furrow you cut is a word to {god}. Some of you have been saying rude things to {god} all {season.now}.
  (faith=land) The {crop} comes up whether we pray or not. We pray so that we notice.
  (faith=land) When you take {material} from the ground, put something back: a seed, a {vessel.one.n} of water, a thank-you.
  (faith=land) {mountain} is {god}'s shoulder and {river} is {god}'s arm, and we live in the crook of it.
  (faith=sky) {skyone} crosses the sky every day without complaint. Some of you cannot cross the street to see your mother.
  (faith=sky) The stars over {mountain} were there before {city} and will be there after it. That is not sad. That is what stars are for.
  (faith=sky) The sky does not hurry and does not stop. Be like the sky, and less like the {herd}.
  (faith=sky moons>0) {moon} keeps its round, and we keep our feasts by it. Keep them on the right nights, at least.
  (faith=sky) {god} hung the lights so that we could count the nights. Count them well; there are not as many as you think.
  (faith=sky) On days of {weather}, when the sky is hidden, {god} is still there, behind it, like a neighbour behind a wall.
  (faith=manygods) {god} is first of the gods, but not the only one listening. Be careful what you say among the {crop}.
  (faith=manygods) Every house has its god and every well, and {god} over all of them, like a mother over loud children.
  (faith=manygods) The god of the {craft}-house is small and bad-tempered. Give it its due, and do not boast in front of it.
  (faith=manygods) There is a god of the {herd} and a god of the road and a god of lost things. Most of you have prayed to the last one over a lost {med.wi.tool}.
  (faith=manygods) Do not set the gods against each other with your prayers; they talk, and they will know.
  (faith=manygods) {god} does not mind the little gods; {god} minds when you forget whose house it is.
  (faith=onegod) There is one {god}, and {god} is not in a hurry; {god} grew the {tree} by {holyplace}, one ring a year.
  (faith=onegod) {god} does not need your offerings. You need to give them. That is the difference.
  (faith=onegod) {god} is one, and gives the whole of that one attention to every soul at {$home}, even the ones at the back. Be worth it.
  (faith=onegod) {god} sees the {med.chr.trades} at the {craft} and the priest at the lamp, and does not think the priest the better.
  (faith=onegod) You cannot bargain with {god} as you bargain for {crop} at {$seat}. {god} has seen the scales.
  (faith=philosophy) The teaching does not ask you to believe. It asks you to look at the {crop}, and then to look again.
  (faith=philosophy) The teaching says: do the next thing well. Not the thing after. The next one.
  (faith=philosophy) The teaching says the {herd} are content because they do not compare. Stop comparing your {vessel}.
  (faith=philosophy) The teaching was written by people, and it says so on the first page. Read it as you would a letter from an aunt who keeps {herd} and knows things.
  (faith=philosophy) The teaching says a quarrel is a fire that warms nobody. Some of you have been warming yourselves all {season.now}.
  (rite=silence) We keep silence because the world is loud enough without us, and the {herd} louder.
  (rite=silence) In the silence you will hear the {bird}, and your own breath, and the thing you have been not thinking about.
  (rite=fast) We fast so that we remember what full is.
  (rite=fast) The fast is not for {god}; {god} is not hungry. It is for the {food} on the table after, and the people round it.
  (rite=song) We sing because some things cannot be said and must be said anyway.
  (rite=song) The old songs are older than {city}. When you sing them you are as old as that.
  (rite=pilgrimage) We go up to {holyplace} not because {god} is there and not here, but because the going changes us.
  (rite=pilgrimage) The road to {holyplace} is the same road every year, and every year it is a different walker.
  (rite=fire) The fire was lit by our first mothers and has not gone out. Do not be the one who lets it.
  (rite=fire) We feed the fire with {fuel} and the fire feeds us with light. That is the whole of the faith, some days.
  (rite=water) We pour the water because water is what we are, mostly, and what we will be.
  (rite=water) The water we pour at {holyplace} comes from {watersrc} and goes back to it. So do we.
  (rite=memory) We say the names of the dead so that the dead are not alone, and neither are we.
  (rite=memory) There are more names every {festival}. Learn them; they are your street.
  (rite=firstfruits) We give {god} the first of the {crop} so that we remember whose it was.
  (rite=firstfruits) The first of the {fruit} goes to {holyplace}, and the second to the poorest house. The rest is yours, and it tastes better for it.
  (rite=animals) We give {god} the firstborn of the {herd} because the first of anything is the hardest to give.
  (rite=animals) The {herd} that go up to {holyplace} go up quietly. Learn from them.

# a story told for a lesson
med.ser.story
  There was once {med.ser.who} at {med.ser.where} who {med.ser.did}. {med.ser.then} {med.ser.moral}
  {med.ser.who.cap} at {med.ser.where} {med.ser.did}. {med.ser.then} {med.ser.moral}

med.ser.who
  a {med.chr.trades.one}
  a woman who kept {herd}
  an old man who made {craft}
  a girl who sold {fruit}
  a rich man with {#nbig} {herd}
  a priest who could not sing
  a boy who would not learn the {craft}
med.ser.who.cap
  A {med.chr.trades.one} I knew
  An old woman I buried last {season.any}
  A man of my own family
  A girl who used to sit where you are sitting
med.ser.where
  {city2}
  {$home}
  the far end of {$home}
  the village under {mountain}
  (coast=1) a harbour I will not name
  a house by {town.edge}
med.ser.did
  kept {med.ser.kept} in a {vessel.one.n} and would not open it, not even at {festival}
  gave away {med.ser.gave} every year, and was laughed at
  counted the {herd} every night, twice, and slept badly
  built a house of {walls.of} bigger than anyone's and lived in one room of it
  worked the {craft} from first light to the bell and never once looked up at {skyone}
  borrowed {tool} from a neighbour and kept it, and the neighbour said nothing for {#n|year}
  (desert=1) dug a cistern for the whole street and charged nobody
  (desert=0) planted {fruit} for the next people and never ate one
med.ser.kept
  the best of the {crop}
  {#n} {coin}
  {crop2} seed
  her mother's {garment.n}
med.ser.gave
  the first of the {crop}
  half the {fruit}
  a {beast} to the poorest house
  bread and salt to every stranger
med.ser.then
  When the bad year came, it was the other way round from what you think, and the {vessel} told it.
  When {med.ser.who.end} died, the whole street came to {deadplace}, and nobody had been asked.
  In the end it all went to the {pest}, which is where most hoarding goes.
  One {season.any} it changed, and nobody knows why, unless the {herd} know.
  It did not go well at first, and then the {crop} came in, and it did.
  The neighbours said nothing for {#n|year}, and then said it all at {festival}, over the {drink}.
  It ended, as these things end, at {town.open}, with everybody watching.

med.ser.who.end
  that one
  the old fool
  that good woman
  that stubborn man
med.ser.moral (share=0.4)
  I will not tell you what it means; you know, and so do the {herd}.
  Go home and think which of them you are, while you eat your {food}.
  That is the whole sermon; the rest is singing, and the {drink}.
  {sermon.said} {proverb}.
  {god} saw it. {god} sees you.
  Remember it when you count your {vessel}.
  The {med.chr.trades} of {$home} tell it the other way round. They are wrong.
  Tell it to your children over the {food}, and let them argue.
  Next {festival} I will tell you what happened to the {herd}.
  The {bird} on the roof heard it too; ask them.

)VG"
